你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。

每个参考题描述包含：
- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。
- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。
- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。
- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。

参考题目如下：
<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="8a6d367c" lines="52-67">
<description>
Complete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.

Input structure:
- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.
- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.
- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.

Rule:
- Crop to the rectangular frame.
- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.
- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.

Key insight:
The lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="25e02866" lines="14-31">
<description>
Merge same-sized filled rectangular objects by overlaying their internal marker pixels.

Input structure:
- A dominant background surrounds two or more separated 4-connected foreground objects.
- Every object is a filled rectangle of the same size.
- Each rectangle has the same majority base color plus one or more non-base marker pixels.

Rule:
- Create one output rectangle with the shared object size, filled with the shared base color.
- For each input rectangle, copy every non-base marker pixel to the same relative location
  in the output.
- Marker overlays must either land on base color or agree in color with an existing marker.

Key insight:
The separated rectangles are layers of the same template; their colored marker pixels
combine by local coordinates while the repeated base fill is just the output canvas.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="2ccd9fef" lines="173-187">
<description>
Predict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.

Input structure
- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.
- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.
- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.

Rule
- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.
- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.
- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.

Key insight
- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="ac6f9922" lines="18-40">
<description>
Summarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.

Input structure
- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot
  backgrounds.
- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of
  slots, ordered by their positions.
- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.

Rule
- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch
  the input edge.
- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output
  table.
- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise
  output the background color.

Key insight
- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are
  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary
  value.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="538b439f" lines="122-142">
<description>
Reflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel
  separator line in a non-background color.
- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.
- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.

Rule:
- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.
- Mirror each source object across the separator line.
- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the
  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the
  reflected object in the source color.

Key insight:
- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified
  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while
  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="d59b0160" lines="39-54">
<description>
Delete connected components whose colors match a legend swatch.

Input structure:
- Background (bg) fills most of the grid.
- Top-left corner: a small square legend region bordered on its bottom and right edges by a single color; the interior holds a set of "target" colors.
- Elsewhere: multicolored objects (4-connected components) on the bg.

Rule:
- Read the target color set from inside the legend border.
- For each connected component, check whether it contains **all** target colors.
- Erase (fill with bg) every component that does; leave all others unchanged.
- Output grid is the same size.

Key insight: The legend acts as a filter specification — only components carrying the full color palette shown in the legend are removed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="ac0c2ac3" lines="7-19">
<description>
Fill each centered square ring with the color of the marker at that ring distance.

Input structure
The input is an odd square grid with a dominant background color and one
non-background marker for every Chebyshev distance from the grid center.
Rule
The output replaces the background with concentric square rings centered on
the middle cell; every cell at distance d from the center takes the color of
the unique marker whose distance from the center is d.
Key insight
The scattered markers are not objects to move; their Chebyshev distances
form a palette for the centered square rings.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="e3f79277" lines="8-23">
<description>
Replace a corner L with a same-color right-triangle outline in the corresponding corner of an enlarged canvas.

Input structure:
- The input is a square grid with one background color and one foreground color.
- The foreground is a one-pixel-thick L inside a square bbox.
- The L touches exactly one horizontal grid edge and one vertical grid edge, identifying a corner.

Rule:
- Fill a square output canvas of side 3n-2, where n is the input side length, with the background color.
- Double the L leg length to get the triangle side length.
- Draw the two perpendicular triangle legs on the same output corner edges and connect their free endpoints with the diagonal.

Key insight:
- The input L encodes the corner, color, and side length of the triangle outline; the missing side is the diagonal between the L's two free endpoints.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="221dfab4" lines="6-19">
<description>
Propagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.
- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.

Rule:
- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.
- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.
- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.

Key insight: Green stripes act as a "flood" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="4c7dc4dd" lines="7-21">
<description>
Learn a transformation rule from a training pair of framed sprites, then apply it to a test sprite.

Input structure:
- Grid contains several colored square frames (hollow borders of a single color).
- Each frame's interior holds a small pattern sprite; one frame's interior is blank (all black).
- Two frames form a training pair (their interiors share the same color palette); one remaining frame is the test case.

Rule:
- Identify the training pair by matching interior color sets; the other non-blank frame is the test input.
- Infer the rule mapping source→destination (e.g. reflection, color inversion, gap filling, endpoint extraction, or checkerboard complement).
- Apply that same rule to the test sprite; output the transformed interior.

Key insight: The puzzle is an analogy — "A is to B as C is to ?" — where you must discover the visual rule from one example pair and generalize it.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="825aa9e9" lines="111-125">
<description>
Drop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.

Input structure:
- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.
- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.
- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.

Rule:
- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.
- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.
- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.

Key insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="898e7135" lines="7-20">
<description>
Inflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.
- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.

Rule:
- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.
- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.
- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.

Key insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="fc7cae8d" lines="9-21">
<description>
Align a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.

Input structure:
- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).
- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.

Rule:
- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.
- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.

Key insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="train" puzzle_id="9344f635" lines="15-30">
<description>
Extend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.
- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.

Rule:
- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.
- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.
- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.

Key insight:
- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="15" source="eval" puzzle_id="16b78196" lines="6-20">
<description>
Assemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid "bar" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).
- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.

Rule:
- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.
- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.
- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.
- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.

Key insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="16" source="train" puzzle_id="15660dd6" lines="33-54">
<description>
Combine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.

Input structure:
- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.
- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.
- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,
  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on
  the background.

Rule:
- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.
- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.
- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask
  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one
  background column.

Key insight:
The table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies
the paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply
the shape mask.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="17" source="train" puzzle_id="278e5215" lines="51-66">
<description>
Recolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.

Input structure:
- The background is the most common color.
- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.
- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.

Rule:
- Crop to the mask object's bbox.
- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.
- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.

Key insight:
The grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="18" source="train" puzzle_id="95755ff2" lines="45-60">
<description>
Paint straight rays from colored side markers into the hollow diamond frame until an interior foreground pixel blocks each ray.

Input structure
- The background contains one complete one-pixel-thick diamond frame.
- Colored marker pixels outside the diamond sit on the top, bottom, left, or right side of the frame's bounding box.
- Any non-background, non-frame pixels inside the diamond are blockers that remain unchanged.

Rule
- Each outside marker emits inward perpendicular to its side of the diamond bounding box.
- Along that row or column, paint only background pixels strictly inside the diamond with the marker color.
- Stop a ray when it reaches an existing interior foreground pixel; keep the frame and all original colored pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The diamond frame defines a strict interior, while the side markers are column or row emitters whose color fills the visible interior interval until blocked.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="19" source="train" puzzle_id="3cd86f4f" lines="6-18">
<description>
Shear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.

Input structure
A rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.

Rule
Create a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy
each input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.

Key insight
The colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so
the left edge forms a descending diagonal.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="20" source="train" puzzle_id="6ffe8f07" lines="49-64">
<description>
Cast yellow horizontal and vertical rays from the teal rectangle until blue blockers stop them.

Input structure:
- A black-background grid containing one solid teal rectangle.
- Blue objects are blockers; other non-background objects may lie in the ray paths.
- Yellow is absent from the input and is the output paint color.

Rule:
- From every row of the teal rectangle, cast rays left and right to the grid edge.
- From every column of the teal rectangle, cast rays up and down to the grid edge.
- A ray stops before a blue pixel; all crossed non-blue, non-teal pixels become yellow.

Key insight:
The teal rectangle defines a full cross-shaped projection, while blue objects are walls and red/background cells are simply paintable space.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

